JOINT STRENGTH REVIEW

Do not globally increase strength by default.

First verify stress-free geometry.

Then verify whether the existing joint profiles can support the ordinary mass of
their intended connected pieces.

A basic Roof Peak and lower corner joint should tolerate:

- The supported weight of one ordinary rafter
- Small settling forces
- Modest compression from a basic roof

They should remain vulnerable to:

- Horizontal spreading forces from unstable supports
- Tension
- Excessive bending
- Direct projectile impact
- Added heavy load
- Loss of one support

If a minimal strength adjustment is necessary after eliminating artificial
strain:

- Change the centralized profile only.
- Document before and after values.
- Justify the adjustment with measured ordinary-load forces.
- Rerun projectile-impact and unsupported-roof tests.

Do not tune based on visual preference alone.

